WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.840
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.870
So in this lecture, we
talk about nuclear fission.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.320
Already seen the process
when we discussed

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.680
empirical mass formula.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.350
Nuclear fission occurs
in very heavy nuclei,

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.620
as you can see in this plot
here, fission processes,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.210
and this part of the spectrum.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.220
But what happens, and what
can happen spontaneously,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.860
is that the parent nuclei
just simply breaks up

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.270
into daughter nuclei and maybe
some additional neutrons.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:47.170
One example is the
decay of uranium 238.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.360
One can also induce fission.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.710
Here, is a plot of the nuclear
potential with the Coulomb

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:54.880
[? law ?] here.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.990
And for example, you have
this nuclei which sits here--

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.290
and this could be uranium 238--

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:07.370
and you are able to bring
it above this activation

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.920
and energy.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:13.600
Again, this can, in some nuclei,
occur spontaneously, in others,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.800
it's being induced.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.320
And then you just
break up the two,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.390
break up the nuclei into
two daughter particles.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.462
So if you, for example,
start with a neutron

00:01:23.462 --> 00:01:24.920
and you just bring
it close-- let's

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:30.240
say you bring a neutron
close to uranium 235.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.870
This forms uranium 236.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.210
And the fact that the neutron
is being absorbed excites,

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.350
then, this zero-kinetic
energy neutron

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.860
excite the daughter
compound nucleus.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.140
There's an excitation
[INAUDIBLE] in this case,

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.960
of 6.5 MeV.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.050
And because of that, it then
quickly undergoes fission.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:55.250
So we basically add its thermal,
or zero-kinetic energy neutron,

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.900
and when it's being
absorbed it immediately

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.110
causes the fission process.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.080
The fission fragments then
carry away some energy--

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.380
in this example, here,
it's about 180 MeV--

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.700
and additional prompt neutrons.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.460
And so those additional
prompt neutrons,

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.270
depending on the
specific decay process,

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.760
the number can be varying
between zero and six,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.820
and for uranium 235, the
average number is 2.5.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.340
And then the
fragments, they might

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:30.320
undergo additional decay
process, maybe better decays,

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.570
alpha decays.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.230
And when they do
that, they can also

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.990
release additional neutrons.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.540
So interesting, now, is
to see whether or not

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.110
there can be a self-sustained
reaction, a chain reaction.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.690
And whether or not this
occurs depends on the number

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:49.480
of neutrons being emitted.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.580
So if the number of
neutrons produced

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:55.570
in the n plus first stage
of the fission process

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.000
is greater than the number of
neutrons produced in the nth

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.720
stage of the
process, the process

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.760
is either critical
or super critical,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.490
which means that it is able
to, because it produces more

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.110
neutrons and it
needs to continue

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:11.920
to have a fission process,
it will add or create

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:12.910
a chain reaction.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:17.800
If the number is less than
1, the process will die out.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:22.480
And this is exactly what's used
in nuclear fission reactors.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.060
There's several types
of reactors available.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.710
The example I want
to discuss here

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.860
very briefly is the one
of a thermal reactor which

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.730
uses uranium as fuel
and low-energy neutrons

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.260
to establish the chain
reaction, as we just discussed.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.200
And so this is a sketch here.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.610
And the sketch has three
different elements.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.520
The first one is a fuel element.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.140
The fuel element can be
naturally-occurring uranium.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.960
And then, we have a
moderator material.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.540
And the purpose of
the moderator material

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.580
is to slow the neutrons down.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.040
So if you really start from
naturally-occurring uranium,

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:12.710
you might have to find
some sort of material which

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.620
allows you to efficiently
slow down the neutrons

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:19.820
when they're being emitted
in the fusion process.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.280
And this could be,
so-called, heavy water,

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.550
where the hydrogen in
water is replaced in part

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.590
that leaves this deuterium.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:33.630
But there's other
examples for this as well.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:37.130
And then, you have those very
important retractable rods.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.820
They are materials which
have a large cross-section

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.800
to capture neutrons.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.300
And so what you can do with
them by mechanically adding them

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.930
or removing them from
this environment,

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.410
is you can remove
additional neutrons.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:03.570
So what you're trying to do
is control this number k here,

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.930
control whether or not there's
enough neutrons available

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.960
in order to sustain
the chain reaction.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.490
And then, the excess energy
is converted in heat.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.890
You can, for example, heat
up water and then just

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:20.810
have a turbine run in
order to produce energy.

